# ApexTrace

**Driving signatures from public Formula 1 telemetry: a signal-processing research project.**

[![tests](https://github.com/SadMan-ZaMaN/ApexTrace/actions/workflows/tests.yml/badge.svg)](https://github.com/SadMan-ZaMaN/ApexTrace/actions/workflows/tests.yml)

![The ApexTrace overview page: two 2024 Abu Dhabi pole laps replayed on a track drawn from the timing feed](docs/images/overview.png)

Can you recognise a Formula 1 driver from the public timing feed alone, and how much of a driver's
"style" belongs to the driver rather than the car?

ApexTrace measures where each driver brakes, how much speed they carry to the slowest point and when they
get back on the throttle, in every braking zone of every qualifying session from 2023 to 2025. It uses only
public timing data, sampled about {{ data.rate_hz }} times a second. The 2025 season was locked away from the first day
and used once, for a preregistered final test.

## Results

| | Question | Result | Verdict |
|---|---|---|---|
| H1 | Are corner features repeatable? | lap-level ICC {{ h1.lap_min }}–{{ h1.lap_max }}, weekend-level {{ h1.weekend_min }}–{{ h1.weekend_max }} | partly |
| H2 | Do two teammates differ by more than the measurement error? (pilot) | {{ pilot.discoveries }} of {{ pilot.tests }} comparisons | not shown: too few laps, as predicted |
| H3 | Can corner features tell teammates apart on weekends the model never saw? | 2025: {{ h3.final.ours }} vs {{ h3.final.lap_time }} for lap time, {{ h3.final.wins }} of {{ h3.final.teams }} teams, {{ h3.final.p }} | **supported**, replicated on the held-out season |
| H4 | Does the style follow drivers who change teams? | 2025: {{ h4.final.own_closer }} of {{ h4.final.changers }} team changers, {{ h4.final.p }} | not supported |

In plain words:

- **Inside a team there is a measurable driver signature.** Using only how two teammates take their corners, a model
  tells them apart on unseen 2025 weekends with a balanced accuracy of {{ h3.final.ours }} (0.5 = guessing), clearly better
  than lap time alone. From one year to the next, a driver's season signature stays closer to their own previous
  season than to their teammate's in the same car ({{ h4.dev.own_closer }} of {{ h4.dev.pairs }} drivers, {{ h4.dev.p }}).
- **It does not beat simple whole-lap statistics** ({{ h3.final.raw }}), such as the share of the lap spent braking.
  What the corner features add is that they are interpretable: they say *where* two drivers differ.
- **Whether the signature survives a change of car was not shown.** Only {{ h4.final.own_closer }} of {{ h4.final.changers }} drivers who moved
  in 2025 stayed closer to their own past than to their new teammate. Drivers who stayed in their team did so
  {{ h4.explore.control_own }} times out of {{ h4.explore.control_n }} in the same comparison, but with five changers the difference can't be told
  from chance ({{ h4.explore.fisher_p }}). The car, or a driver adapting to it, matters a lot.

The full write-up is in [docs/report.md](docs/report.md). The test plan was committed before it was run:
[docs/preregistration.md](docs/preregistration.md) (commits `{{ prereg.part_a }}` and `{{ prereg.part_b }}`), and every deviation
is in [docs/preregistration_deviations.md](docs/preregistration_deviations.md).

